body:
Just tried updating to 2.5.0 and hit this as well.

In my case the Assert.Equal is now failing on Dictionary<string,object>, where some cases the object is a nested Dictionary<string,object> recursively.

Example:

```
var dictionary = new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) {
    { "int", 1 },
    { "string", "two" },
    { "bool", true },
    { "nested1Root", new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) {
        { "int", 11 },
        { "string", "two-two" },
        { "bool", false },
        { "nested1Middle", new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) {
            { "int", 111 },
            { "string", "two-two-two" },
            { "bool", true },
            { "nested1End", new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) {
                { "int", 1111 },
                { "string", "two-two-two-two" },
                { "bool", false },
            }},
        }},
    }},
    { "nested2Root", new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) {
        { "int", 99 },
        { "string", "nine-nine" },
        { "bool", true },
    }},
    { "anotherRoot", "value" }
};
```

Rolling back to 2.4.x and hope the fix coming up in 2.5.1 addresses my scenario as well.

@bradwilson
It looks like we still have regressions in 2.5.1. The following test is failing now but passing with 2.4.2. Should I create a new issue or will you take care of it from here?

```        [Fact]
        public void TestEqualDict()
        {
            var dict1 = new Dictionary<string, object>();
            dict1["key"] = new Dictionary<string, object>()
            {
                {
                    "key", new List<Dictionary<string, object>>()
                        {
                            new Dictionary<string, object>()
                            {
                                {
                                    "key", new List<object> { "value" }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                }
            };
            var dict2 = new Dictionary<string, object>();
            dict2["key"] = new Dictionary<string, object>()
            {
                {
                    "key", new List<Dictionary<string, object>>()
                        {
                            new Dictionary<string, object>()
                            {
                                {
                                    "key", new List<object> { "value" }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                }
            };
            Assert.Equal(dict1, dict2);
        }
```
